# Agent Workflow

- While experimenting or debugging, it is fine to move quickly.
- Before creating or updating the PR for review, do a cleanup pass over the diff.
- Cleanup pass rules:
- Write extremely simple code; it should be skimmable and still understandable at a glance.
- Minimize possible states by reducing argument count and narrowing state.
- Use discriminated unions to reduce the number of states the code can be in.
- Exhaustively handle objects with multiple different types and fail on unknown types.
- Do not write defensive code for impossible states; trust the types.
- Use asserts when loading data and when something is expected to exist.
- Do not make things optional unless they are strictly optional.
- Do not pass overrides unless they are strictly necessary.
- Remove any changes that are not strictly required.
- Bias toward fewer lines of code.
- Prefer early returns.
- Avoid complex, clever, or over-factored code.
- This cleanup pass only needs to happen when the branch is about to go into a PR. It does not need to constrain exploratory or debugging code before then.
- If the cleanup pass changes how code should be run or verified, update the experiment log with the final reproducible commands and results before updating the PR.

# Agent-Owned Cursor Files

- The agent may edit `.cursor/environment.json`, `.cursor/Dockerfile` if this repo later adds one, and files under `.cursor/skills/`.
- Only make those edits for durable, reusable, high-leverage improvements.
- Do not change agent-owned files for one-off task-specific convenience.
- If the agent edits its own environment or skills, add an `Agent Requests` section to the PR explaining what changed and why.
- If the agent has a general capability request or tooling request, add an `Agent Requests` section to the PR describing what is needed and why it is high leverage.

# Shared Memory

- Keep durable, long-term memory in `.cursor/memories.md`.
- Use that file for high-leverage information that should survive across different agents or different runs.
- Record rejected or deferred high-leverage capability requests there so future agents do not ask for the same thing every time.
- Do not store transient debugging notes or one-off task context there.
